<Project>

  <!--
    THE VERSION LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE (HM-DEC-063).

    Every project in the solution reads it from this one file, so the About
    window, the telemetry line and the assembly all report the same number and
    none of them can drift from the others. About reads it off the assembly at
    run time rather than carrying a string of its own (HM-DEC-019), which is
    what makes one place enough.

    Semantic versioning, with the meanings §1 of CLAUDE.md records: major for a
    change that breaks somebody's existing setup or data, minor for a new
    capability the operator can see and use, patch for fixes and polish that add
    none.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.27 -> 1.12.28, UNIT 221, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH BECAUSE NOTHING AN OPERATOR COULD SEE
    MOVED: no Hamlet file changed, no Ft8Sharp file changed except porting-notes.md, and the night's
    whole product is a measurement. Step 6's sensitivity curve was taken for the first time in the
    phase - criteria 1 and 3 met, criterion 2 NOT MET at 13 of 306 at -21 dB and recorded as
    HM-OPEN-067.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.28 -> 1.12.29, UNIT 222, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH FOR THE SAME REASON AND IT IS THE SIXTH
    RUNNING: no Hamlet file changed, no Ft8Sharp file changed except porting-notes.md, and the
    night's whole product is a measurement. The 1.5 dB shortfall was taken apart stage by stage - the
    decibel axis checked against an independently built second instrument and holding to 0.04 dB, and
    five oracle substitutions at -21 dB every one of which lands inside the as-is rate's own interval.
    NO STAGE HOLDS THE SHORTFALL. bp_decode audited against the pin at thirteen of fourteen terms
    identical, so the port is faithful and criterion 2 stays NOT MET at 13 of 306. HM-OPEN-067 updated
    in place with the budget.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.29 -> 1.12.30, UNIT 223, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH FOR THE SAME REASON AND IT IS THE SEVENTH
    RUNNING: no Hamlet file changed, no Ft8Sharp file changed except porting-notes.md, and the
    night's whole product is a measurement. The one budget item unit 222 could not price was priced -
    upstream's fast_tanh and fast_atanh replaced by the real functions in the test project, worth two
    decodes in 306 - and the question underneath the whole phase was measured instead of inferred: an
    independently written soft decoder over the identical ratios also lands inside the as-is
    interval, and over every failing trial THE TRUE CODEWORD SCORES LOWER THAN THE WORD THE DECODER
    SETTLED ON. The information is not in the ratios, and criterion 2 stays NOT MET at 13 of 306.
    HM-OPEN-067 updated in place.

    1.12.31 -> UNIT 224, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH, AND THE FIRST OF THIS PHASE THAT CHANGES WHAT THE
    OPERATOR SEES. The Digital tab's decoded table stops being four literal strings from work
    instruction 037 and becomes whatever the band was doing when the capture button was pressed:
    Hamlet.RadioEngine references Ft8Sharp for the first time, a band-limited resampler puts the sound
    card's own rate onto the 12 kHz grid the library was measured at, and the slot cutter that has sat
    unused since August cuts the capture on the UTC quarter minute. Measured through four sample rates
    including one that is not a whole ratio.

    1.12.32 -> UNIT 225, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH, AND THE ONE THAT TAKES THE PRESS OUT OF THE LOOP.
    Unit 224 left the count of slots decoded with nobody pressing anything at nought; FT8 opens a new
    slot every fifteen seconds, so an operator pressing a button for each one is sampling a band
    rather than watching it. Ft8SlotWatch in the engine notices when a quarter minute has closed and
    hands over exactly that slot's audio - clock passed in, never read, so the alignment is assertable
    against a controllable clock. The Digital tab rides the decode timer, appends rather than
    replaces, bounds the table, shows nothing twice and clears on a band change. The capture press now
    contributes to the same table instead of wiping it. No library file changed.

    1.12.33 -> UNIT 226, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH, AND THE ONE WHERE THE APPLICATION FIRST HEARS A
    REAL BAND. Every row the Digital tab has ever shown came from audio this project synthesized for
    itself, and a port that is self-consistently wrong at both ends passes every such test. Karlis
    Goba's off-air recordings now go through Hamlet's own ring buffer at 48 000 and 44 100 in the 480
    and 441 sample chunks a shared-mode capture delivers, are cut by the same slot watch that runs on
    the tab, and come out as rows: 194 out of twelve slots at each rate, fifteen of them read back off
    the view model's own collection. The slowest slot through the whole path is 326.0 ms, which is
    0.0217 of a slot. No product code changed - the change is a shared test-side locator for the
    recordings, two measurement classes and BENCH_CHECK.md. No library file changed.

    1.12.34 -> UNIT 227, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH, AND THE ONE THAT SETTLES WHERE THE SENSITIVITY
    SHORTFALL CAME FROM. Three units opened, substituted and priced every stage inside this receiver
    without moving step 6's criterion 2 off 13 of 306. Unit 227 pointed the first instrument from
    OUTSIDE it: upstream's own decode_ft8.exe, built from the pin by the owner's own oracle script,
    reading the IDENTICAL slots - each written to disk once as a 12 kHz sixteen-bit WAV and read by
    both decoders in turn. Over three rungs and 918 paired slots the two agree on every one: 14 and
    14 at -21 dB, 71 and 71 at -20, 248 and 248 at -19, with an empty off-diagonal and 0 wrong on
    both sides throughout. The shortfall was inherited with the code. On the way past, upstream's
    decoder read 51 of 51 signals this library synthesized, which discharges HM-OPEN-065 after
    seventeen units. Nothing was tuned, raised or adopted; the deliberate-divergence question is the
    owner's. No file under src/ changed except this one and porting-notes.md.

    1.12.35 -> UNIT 228, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH BECAUSE PHASE_PLAN.md RESERVES THE MINOR FOR WHEN
    THIS PHASE CLOSES, and it is otherwise the first change since unit 224 that an operator will see
    without being told where to look. The Digital tab gains one readiness line, above every panel and
    outside the scroller, that names the FIRST thing standing between him and a decode: nothing
    listening, the training radio as the source, a clock that is out or has never been checked, a
    radio in the wrong mode or one that has not said, or a dial outside the digital blocks. It is
    EMPTY AND THE WHOLE ROW IS GONE when none of those is wrong, and a test asserts that, because a
    line that always says something is one the operator stops reading. Hamlet knew all five of these
    already and said none of them in one place, so a blank decoded table could not be told from a
    wrong setup. The mode strip beside it stops being four literals: FT8 was lit in the markup
    wherever the dial was, and which chip lights is now the map's answer. One claim the received
    wisdom would have written is deliberately not written - Ft8SlotCutter cuts on the measured offset
    rather than on the machine's own minute, so a clock that is out does not stop this program
    decoding, and the line says how far out it is instead of sending the operator to fix what was
    already handled. RigState gained a fallback to the last state through its one seam so the mode
    condition could be reached by a test; production behaviour does not move. No decoder, threshold or
    Ft8Sharp file changed.

    1.12.36 -> UNIT 230, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH, AND THE ONE WITH NO PRODUCT CHANGE IN IT AT ALL -
    nothing under src/ moved, no threshold moved, no panel says anything different. What moved is
    that the test suite can now be read. Hamlet.App.Tests had never once been run to completion in
    this repository: unit 228 concluded it passes everything and then will not exit, and that was
    wrong - it STOPS PARTWAY, at 92, 251, 170, 49, 41 and 34 results out of 557 declared across six
    recorded runs, each naming a different test as in flight and each putting exactly the inactivity
    bound between its last result and the abort. Ten runs tonight localised it: the stall lives
    entirely in the Hamlet.App.Tests.Views namespace and depends on how many of those 16 classes
    share one host, not on which. Everything outside Views reads 495 of 495 and returns; Views split
    three ways reads 25, 20 and 17 and returns. 495+25+20+17 is exactly 557, all green, no hang
    collector - the project's first complete run column. It reproduces with all four classes this
    phase added excluded and with Views running alone, so it is INHERITED and not the FT8 phase's.
    No fix was attempted: the seam is cross-class state on the one process-wide Avalonia headless
    dispatcher, and the only production object a reading can name near it is MainWindowViewModel's
    timer lifetime, which is not moving the night before the owner measures the decoder at the radio.
    docs/full-suite-run.md is the deliverable - what he types, what he reads, how long each leg takes,
    what a hang looks like beside a failure, every line tagged measured-tonight or inherited.

    1.12.41 -> UNIT 237, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH BECAUSE IT IS A DEFECT REPAIR AND NOT A NEW
    CAPABILITY, and it is the first defect this phase has found in the segment between the antenna and
    everything else. Every test that has ever shown this application hearing a real band starts from
    mono floats that were already made - RealOffAirAudioReachesTheTabTests says so in its own comment
    at line 311 - so WasapiAudioSource.Downmix and ReadSample, the code that makes those floats out of
    a sound card's bytes, were executed by NO test in this repository at all. ReadSample decided a
    sample was floating point only when the format's top-level tag read IeeeFloat, and Windows
    shared-mode capture commonly presents its mix format as WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE with the real
    answer one level down in a subformat GUID. On such a device every float the radio delivered was
    reinterpreted as a 32-bit integer: loud, structureless, nonlinear, and with every FT8 tone in it
    destroyed - a healthy level meter over an empty table. MEASURED, at 48 000 and at 44 100 alike:
    Karlis Goba's three busiest off-air recordings gave 47 messages through the float path, 24 through
    an extensible-float device buffer, and 47 after the repair. Fourteen rows of the format table are
    now asserted from constructed bytes, mono and stereo with the channels carrying different values.
    The _ => 0.0 arm is gone; an unrecognised format throws, the buffer is dropped, and nothing
    reaches the tap, because inventing silence is the one answer no reading downstream can tell from a
    dead band (HM-DEC-009). AND THE HONEST QUALIFIER: whether this is what silenced the bench check of
    2026-09-03 is NOT KNOWN and cannot be established from this tree. SHACK_FACTS.md FACT-004, added
    during unit 237, records that the radio lives on a different computer and that nothing measured
    about the development machine's audio endpoints says anything about it - so the two endpoints
    enumerated here, both IeeeFloat 32-bit, neither clear nor implicate this defect. The question is
    one row on one capture sheet on the shack machine and it is in unit 237's report. No file under
    src/Ft8Sharp/ changed.

    1.12.52 -> UNIT 251, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH BECAUSE PHASE_PLAN.md RESERVES THE MINOR FOR WHEN
    THIS PHASE CLOSES, and it is the second change of the phase an operator will see without being
    told where to look. THE snr COLUMN STOPS BEING A DASH. It has carried one since work instruction
    037, which committed the column on the assumption that a signal-to-noise ratio is what a decoder
    produces; it is not - Ft8Sharp returns a Costas sync score - and DigitalDecodeRow has said so in
    prose for two hundred units while showing an em dash.

    Ft8Sharp.Deep 0.4.0 -> 0.5.0 gains Ft8DeepSignalToNoise, which reads the power in the tone that
    was transmitted against the seven tones that were not, at the same instant, over the symbols
    whose windows lie inside the slot, and carries the per-bin ratio to the 2500 Hz reference
    bandwidth by 10 log10(2500 / 6.25) = 26.0206 dB. That constant is DERIVED from the tone spacing
    and the symbol period - the decimation, the filter length and the sample rate all cancel - and
    NOTHING IN THIS UNIT IS FITTED TO THE LADDER. The symbol sequence it needs is packed back out of
    the decoded message, guarded by a round trip through the message layer, because the decode result
    hands back text and carries no bits.

    MEASURED, and this is what the column shows a number on: over 510 synthesized messages, five
    rungs from -18 to -6 dB and two placements, through the decoder Hamlet actually runs, the
    estimate agreed with the ratio the ladder delivered to a MEAN ABSOLUTE ERROR OF 0.26 dB and a
    95th PERCENTILE OF 0.62 dB, with 510 of 510 trials decoding so nothing is selected. PHASE_PLAN.md
    says the dash stays if agreement is worse than 2 dB. It is not.

    Whole decibels in the column, because one message in twenty is 0.62 dB or further out and a first
    decimal would be noise drawn as signal. The tooltip carries the 95th percentile as well as the
    mean. The dash did not go away - it now means a message whose ratio could not be measured, which
    is a null and never a floor.

    The number reaches all three surfaces with one meaning: the panel cell at the 48-pixel width
    reserved for it since 037, four snr fields on the per-slot telemetry line, and per-message lines
    on the capture sidecar. Telemetry gets a spread and not a message, because AppEvents is handed
    the census and never the reception and HM-DEC-018 keeps decoded content out of that file.

    REPORT-ONLY, asserted rather than claimed: decoding a slot again after the estimate has been
    taken returns the identical Ft8SlotResult. No file under src/Ft8Sharp/ changed.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>1.12.53</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE BUILD DATE IS STAMPED AT COMPILE TIME (HM-DEC-079).

    About used to read it off the assembly file's last-write time, which is a
    property of a file copy rather than of a build: an incremental build that
    did not recompile this project, or a copy that preserved timestamps, made it
    report a day that had nothing to do with the code running. It showed
    2026-08-14 while running code built the next day.

    A build date that can be stale is worse than no build date, because it is
    the row somebody uses to check that two machines are running the same code.
    This one comes from the compilation itself and cannot be a copy artifact.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BuildStampUtc>$([System.DateTime]::UtcNow.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm"))</BuildStampUtc>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Reflection.AssemblyMetadataAttribute">
      <_Parameter1>BuildStampUtc</_Parameter1>
      <_Parameter2>$(BuildStampUtc)</_Parameter2>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
